body: "## Problem\n\nA swarm's real limit is often not files or lanes but a shared, windowed capacity: a model provider's weekly quota, a rate-limit window, a pool of CI runners. `pm swarm` can lease an exclusive resource (`pm swarm resource … --action reserve`), but it has no notion of a counted capacity that drains over a window, so:\n\n- `pm swarm next` and `start` hand out lanes that the provider cannot finish. In one week of real orchestration, four long-running agents were stopped 40 to 80 minutes into a task by an exhausted provider window. Each left uncommitted or unpushed work and no record of why it stopped.\n- A stopped lane looks like a stale claim. `reclaim --stale-after` returns it to the pool, and the next agent starts again from the item text instead of from the previous agent's state.\n- The orchestrator's budget rule (\"the forecast at the reset must stay under 100%, so run at most N lanes\") lives in ad-hoc scripts and is invisible to the agents it constrains.\n\n## Proposal: `pm swarm capacity`\n\nCounted, windowed capacities stored as pm items with append-only observations. No provider API is called by the package; the caller reports what it measured.\n\n| command | effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `pm swarm capacity codex --action declare --limit 100 --unit percent --window 7d --resets-at <ISO>` | Declare a capacity and its window |\n| `pm swarm capacity codex --action observe --used 44 --source codexbar` | Append one observation; the read model derives elapsed share, burn rate and forecast at reset |\n| `pm swarm capacity codex` | Status: used, elapsed, forecast, admitted lane count under the policy bands, time to reset |\n| `pm swarm capacity codex --action policy --band \"forecast<88=3\" --band \"forecast<95=2\" --band \"forecast<98=1\" --else 0` | How many lanes may hold this capacity at once |\n| `pm swarm next --agent worker --capacity codex` / `start … --capacity codex` | Admission: bind the lane to the capacity, or refuse with `capacity_forecast_exceeded` or `capacity_exhausted` and name the next capacity in a declared fallback order (`--capacity ollama,codex`) |\n| `pm swarm interrupt task-id --agent worker --token 1 --reason capacity_exhausted --capacity ollama` | Record that the lane stopped for capacity, keep the claim fenced (not stale), and mark the capacity exhausted until its reset |\n| `pm swarm brief task-id --continuation --to worker-2 --token-budget 800` | Continuation packet for a different agent or provider: what the lane changed since the claim (linked files, synced paths, comments and tests in order), what the acceptance criteria still require, and the interruption record. Transfers ownership like `handoff` |\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Works without Git; in Git, the continuation packet also lists the lane's commits and uncommitted paths.\n- Admission is atomic under concurrent `next` calls (two agents cannot take the last admitted slot); proven with a real multi-process test.\n- Forecast is derived from at least two observations in the current window; with fewer it is reported as unknown and the policy's `--else` applies. An observation older than a stated age is reported as stale, never silently used.\n- `reclaim --stale-after` does not reclaim an interrupted lane before its capacity resets or a continuation is taken.\n- Every refusal names the capacity, the measured values, the reset time and one runnable next command.\n- No secrets or provider credentials are read or stored; observations are numbers plus a free-text source label.\n- README section with a worked example, 100% coverage, npm and bun acceptance.\n\n## Why here and not a new package\n\nA standalone budget package was rejected in the 2026-11-04 overlap audit. Capacity is a scheduling input for lanes, next to the existing exclusive `resource` leases, and the continuation packet is a variant of `brief` plus `handoff`.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-11-24T10:28:03Z)\n>\n> <!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n> \n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-swarm#96 - Add budgeted fenced assignment briefs [merged]\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\":\"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> <dl>\n> <dd>\n> <br>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </dd>\n> </dl>\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
